WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.160
Something crazy just happened&nbsp;
to GoPro, and I think a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:09.760
of people missed a very important detail.
Markiplier posted a sponsored GoPro video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.480
and days later, everyone found out&nbsp;
he was GoPro's biggest shareholder.&nbsp;

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:19.920
The stock more than doubled in two days.
Then, out of nowhere, GoPro agreed to merge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.040
with a company almost no one had heard of.
All within about five days.&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:35.280
People were confused, and a bit suspicious.
Some began to ask: “Did Markiplier pump and dump?”&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:39.920
But, I think, Mark might be the least&nbsp;
strange part of this whole thing.&nbsp;

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:42.480
There’s something everyone seemed to miss.&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:47.040
GoPro's margins had fallen&nbsp;
from 32% to 4% in a year.&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.920
That company they merged with was&nbsp;
incorporated the day before the deal.&nbsp;

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.440
And GoPro is staying on the stock market?
What the heck is going on at GoPro?&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.040
So, to understand all these wild events,&nbsp;
let’s go back to that first video.&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.920
On August 27th, Markiplier&nbsp;
uploaded a sponsored GoPro video.&nbsp;

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:41.680
A very normal, inconspicuous&nbsp;
video. But immediately after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.640
a weird series of events took place.
On August 31st, Bloomberg reported that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:53.120
Mark had accumulated an 8.5% stake in GoPro.
Markiplier told Bloomberg, “It blows my mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.280
what it can do in such a small package,”
“They have a big future with this camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:03.440
coming out opening up a new segment for them.”
He also said, “I saw the stock and where it was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:07.920
I was like that seems undervalued. It’s&nbsp;
just something I’ve been cooking in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.920
background; I want the company to succeed.”
Within a day, the media all began sharing it.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.160
“8.5% stake”, “biggest&nbsp;
shareholder”, “Undervalued”.&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.040
This sent the stock into a frenzy.
The stock closed up 46% on August&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:29.040
31st, then another 80% on September 1st.
Then, on September 1st, GoPro announced it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:34.800
be merging, with a company called Starman Optical.
They said “shareholders will receive an aggregate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:43.440
cash payment of $285 million, or $1.14 per share”
At this price, Markiplier will get $15 million.&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.880
Business Insider estimated his&nbsp;
profit at about $5 million.&nbsp;

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:50.880
At this point, a bunch of people began&nbsp;
to notice this pattern of events.&nbsp;

00:02:50.880 --> 00:03:56.400
So… did Markiplier pump &amp; dump everybody?
Mark had been accumulating stock for months.&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:01.840
So no. Not by a long shot, did he pump and dump.
It was July 13th that he had reached the amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:06.240
that triggered a disclosure obligation.
Then, after a lot of processing time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:09.280
his ownership was made public&nbsp;
on August 20th (show last page).&nbsp;

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.160
He also told Business Insider, "I'm not&nbsp;
going to lie, I'm disappointed, [about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:19.120
merger]" "I would vote against the deal."
But, he couldn’t do anything about it.&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.520
He had acquired Class A shares, meaning even&nbsp;
though he was the largest shareholder, he only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:28.880
had a small amount of voting power, compared&nbsp;
to Class B shares, which get 10 votes each.&nbsp;

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:33.280
And when you take a step back… Markiplier&nbsp;
investing in GoPro, makes a lot of sense.&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:38.320
He’s done some extremely ambitious projects&nbsp;
over the years, most of all: Iron Lung.&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:43.280
A professional, self-funded, self-acted,&nbsp;
written, and directed feature-length film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:48.240
which grossed over $50 million at the box&nbsp;
office… with around a $5 million budget.&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.800
Some of the special effects were rendered using&nbsp;
a whole bunch of Mac Studios, in his bathroom.&nbsp;

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.560
This is the story of this funny&nbsp;
photo of him showing Tim Cook&nbsp;

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.080
So, a filmmaker investing in a camera&nbsp;
company, which is also producing more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:06.160
filmmaking equipment, makes a lot of sense.
So, did Mark do anything illegal? No.&nbsp;

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.760
He didn’t pump the stock, and&nbsp;
had no involvement in the merger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.760
and a stock will always jump when word&nbsp;
gets out a big name invests in it.&nbsp;

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:17.440
But… even though the GoPro video&nbsp;
was clearly disclosed as sponsored,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:22.400
he probably should have clearly disclosed that he&nbsp;
was also a GoPro shareholder in the sponsor video.&nbsp;

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:26.160
The FTC states that “if you endorse&nbsp;
a product through social media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:29.520
your endorsement message should make&nbsp;
it obvious when you have a relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:34.000
(“material connection”) with the brand.”
He has this “material connection” to GoPro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:39.200
and directly benefits from the profitability&nbsp;
and perception of the GoPro brand, and thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:43.840
has a higher incentive to showcase that.
Though, this doesn’t prove illegality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:49.280
and he did add it shortly after.
So maybe a slight slip-up, but I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:55.280
see no malicious intent here, nothing illegal.
I genuinely think Mark liked GoPro products,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:59.040
invested, and the news broke,&nbsp;
right as the merger happened.&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:04.640
But, this is actually just the tip of the iceberg.
Some pretty insane things have been going on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:09.760
independent of Mark.
For one, GoPro, has been barely surviving.&nbsp;

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:16.000
In Q1 of 2026, they lost $81&nbsp;
million, on revenue of $99 million!&nbsp;

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:21.600
But the reason is what’s weird. GoPro is&nbsp;
struggling for a very particular reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.920
mostly out of their control.
And that’s not all.&nbsp;

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:31.440
The other “merging” company, was&nbsp;
incorporated on August 31st, 2026…&nbsp;

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:36.400
One day before the merger.
Then merged with GoPro… which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:41.600
still traded as a public company?
So, what really happened?&nbsp;

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:50.240
Well… this wasn’t really a merger,&nbsp;
at all. It’s closer to a “takeover…”

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:57.120
Let’s get a little bit of context.
GoPro’s sudden jump after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:02.320
investment, sure sounds like a lot.
GoPro stock closed 46% higher on Aug 31&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:07.120
and the next day, jumped another 80%.
That sounds huge…&nbsp;

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:12.000
…until you see the bigger picture
The first jump was to 88 cents, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:19.280
that 80% jump… was only to $1.58.
So… 80% of basically nothing.&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:22.880
GoPro, had collapsed, long&nbsp;
before Markiplier invested.&nbsp;

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:25.840
But, it was in a way worse&nbsp;
spot than people realise.&nbsp;

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:30.960
GoPro was once the unicorn of unicorns.
It exploded with a great product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:36.400
an untapped market, and great branding.
But… they gradually fumbled their spot at the top,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:42.720
fell behind, and soon competitors got ahead.
But what matters for us today, is how bad things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:48.480
had gotten, in 2026, and this time,&nbsp;
it’s hard to say it’s GoPro’s fault.&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:52.560
In GoPro’s Q2 financial statements,&nbsp;
they said this fairly candidly.&nbsp;

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.960
“The company’s performance continued to be&nbsp;
adversely impacted by an increasingly global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:00.720
competitive landscape, consumer-related&nbsp;
macroeconomic issues resulting in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:05.200
softer global consumer market, rising&nbsp;
memory costs and supply constraints. ”&nbsp;

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:12.160
Their net loss for the first quarter, was $81&nbsp;
million, on revenue of $99 million. Not good.&nbsp;

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:17.120
In the same quarter last year,&nbsp;
they also had a $47 million loss.&nbsp;

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:21.680
So, why was GoPro struggling?
There’s a multitude of reasons:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:27.920
a softer market, a more saturated market.&nbsp;
But there’s one specific, recent problem.&nbsp;

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:34.560
One of the biggest, is “rising memory costs”.
Their gross margin, aka revenue minus cost of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:39.520
making the product, was just 4.3%.
But the weird part, is that margin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:45.520
was as high as 32.1% a year earlier.
“the Company’s forecast has been significantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:50.640
impacted by [quote continues…] continued increases&nbsp;
and volatility in memory costs, including an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:57.920
initial unexpected price increases ranging from&nbsp;
80% to 115% in the last week of March 2026."&nbsp;

00:08:57.920 --> 00:08:59.592
The CFO also said:
[Brian Tratt. Chief Financial Officer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.592 --> 00:09:05.520
GoPro]: "Memory pricing increased from a range&nbsp;
of 80% to 115% in the last week of the quarter."&nbsp;

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:10.720
In just the last week. Crazy.
GoPro, as you may have guessed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:15.360
doesn’t build its own memory. Very&nbsp;
likely DRAM for its camera processors.&nbsp;

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:20.640
It commits to buying a set quantity in advance,&nbsp;
at a set price, on terms it can't cancel.&nbsp;

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:25.200
In April 2026, GoPro's memory suppliers&nbsp;
told it they were reducing production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:30.880
of the memory used in its products.
But, memory costs rise 80 to 115% in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:37.040
a week. GoPro is stuck. It already promised to buy&nbsp;
a certain amount, for a certain number of cameras.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:42.000
And now it can't afford to make that many.
This is why in Q1, they had to write off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:47.360
a loss of $24.5 million on components&nbsp;
they had ordered and couldn't cancel.&nbsp;

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:52.560
That’s why their gross margin had&nbsp;
fallen almost 90% in one year.&nbsp;

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:57.680
So GoPro built fewer cameras, because there&nbsp;
wasn't enough memory left to build them with.&nbsp;

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:01.360
Memory suppliers were producing&nbsp;
less of the memory GoPro needed.&nbsp;

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:04.400
And can you guess why?
They were putting most of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:09.680
capacity towards memory for data centres.
Still, there was a bit of hope.&nbsp;

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:16.560
Q2 subscription and services revenue was up 11% to&nbsp;
about $29.0 million, and made up 28% of revenue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:20.160
against 17% a year earlier.
That’s great.&nbsp;

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:25.920
But hardware revenue was falling a&nbsp;
lot. From $126 million in Q2 2025,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:32.800
to just $76 million in Q2 2026.
So… why acquire this struggling camera company?&nbsp;

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:37.600
Starman Optical, the company that&nbsp;
merged with GoPro… what did they want?&nbsp;

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:41.840
Well, remember, this company&nbsp;
was incorporated a day before.&nbsp;

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:46.400
This wasn’t really a merger, at all.
And Starman wasn’t here for the cameras.&nbsp;

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:56.240
But something else.
If you’ve watched this far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:01.040
consider subscribing! It helps the channel&nbsp;
and means you won’t miss future videos.&nbsp;

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:08.160
Everyone is talking about a GoPro “merger”,&nbsp;
and legally, it is a “merger”… But it wasn’t.&nbsp;

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:10.480
It was a takeover.
Starman Optical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:16.640
incorporated August 31st, in Delaware, is&nbsp;
actually a subsidiary of Action Acquisitions LLC.&nbsp;

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:21.520
So, a new acquisition shell was formed to&nbsp;
merge with GoPro, which itself disappears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:25.200
as a separate legal company.
But, in reality, this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:30.320
Action Acquisitions, acquiring GoPro.
This is a standard legal mechanism called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:35.280
a reverse subsidiary merger. The buyer&nbsp;
creates a subsidiary, merges it into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:39.920
target, and the target survives.
But GoPro would keep the name.&nbsp;

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:45.600
They’d also… stay a public company?
Not only that, there was a $285 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:52.480
cash payment to GoPro shareholders, at $1.14&nbsp;
per share, hence the profit Markiplier got.&nbsp;

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:57.280
And, there was about $92 million of&nbsp;
GoPro debt to be repaid at closing.&nbsp;

00:11:57.280 --> 00:12:00.400
So why?
Why do all this?&nbsp;

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:05.760
Well, if we look at GoPro’s announcement,&nbsp;
Starman Optical is a “Starman Holding company”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:10.720
and somewhere inside that structure, is “Starman&nbsp;
New Photonics.” This entity is “focused on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:16.400
development and domestic manufacturing of optical&nbsp;
transceivers and related photonics technologies”&nbsp;

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:22.160
All of this is under Action Acquisitions,&nbsp;
although we don’t quite know the exact structure.&nbsp;

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:25.760
Point is, Starman New&nbsp;
Photonics, optical transceivers.&nbsp;

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:28.640
Essential pieces of hardware,&nbsp;
to convert electrical signals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:33.120
into light pulses for fibre-optic cables.
It’s essential in sending huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:38.080
amounts of data, very, very fast.
Does this sound familiar? It should,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:42.320
because there’s something everyone’s&nbsp;
building to process lots and lots of data…&nbsp;

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:46.960
Let’s look at GoPro’s press release again.
“Transaction Expected to Add U.S. Onshore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:52.480
Optical Transceiver Business, Positioning&nbsp;
GoPro to Expand into AI Data Center markets”&nbsp;

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:59.040
“Advanced optics and imaging are essential to AI”.
GoPro was acquired, not because of its cameras,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:04.480
but its optics and imaging technology.&nbsp;
It has 2,500 U.S. patents, many of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:07.760
around this technology.
And that’s not all.&nbsp;

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:12.240
Remember how GoPro is still a public company?
GoPro’s existing shareholders would hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:17.120
about 10% of the company post-merger.
Action Acquisitions/Starman’s owners would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:21.360
hold the other roughly 90%.
But why stay public?&nbsp;

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:25.920
Listing a business on the Nasdaq takes&nbsp;
a lot of time, and costs a lot of money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:30.480
including lots of bank fees, regulatory&nbsp;
filings, marketing, more investor scrutiny…&nbsp;

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:36.160
By merging into GoPro, Starman can gain&nbsp;
control of an already listed company instead.&nbsp;

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:41.520
But doing this means they don’t get&nbsp;
to raise money through an IPO? So why?&nbsp;

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:46.880
Well, it gives owners publicly tradable shares&nbsp;
to sell, and a “stock currency” to compensate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:53.040
employees, raise capital, or buy other companies.
And this is especially important, with them being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:58.080
a photonics/AI-infrastructure company.
A reverse-merger route is often faster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:03.440
and cheaper than an IPO, even if it&nbsp;
doesn’t itself raise new money on day one.&nbsp;

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:09.280
And that’s a part no one seemed to notice.
This wasn’t just Starman merging with GoPro. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:15.120
was Starman skipping an IPO, and turning GoPro’s&nbsp;
trading stock… partly into the Starman stock.&nbsp;

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:19.760
Markiplier voted against the&nbsp;
merger, and I understand why.&nbsp;

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:35.760
GoPro is, but also isn’t really,&nbsp;
a camera company now. Though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:38.800
they definitely won’t be stopping&nbsp;
that side of the business.&nbsp;

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:42.640
But, I don’t think GoPro would&nbsp;
have survived without this merger.&nbsp;

00:14:42.640 --> 00:14:48.080
GoPro’s action-camera category is mature&nbsp;
and fiercely competitive, particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:54.720
against companies such as DJI and Insta360.
AI optics are a much bigger strategic bet.&nbsp;

00:14:54.720 --> 00:15:02.480
The larger question though, is how did GoPro fall&nbsp;
so far behind? Well, that story is even stranger.&nbsp;

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:07.600
A huge IPO, then huge talk of moving beyond&nbsp;
a camera company, to a “media company”…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:11.920
then a strange, total collapse.
To learn the rest of the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:14.880
watch this video next.
Or, if you’d like a video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:20.080
where a single YouTube review seemed to kill&nbsp;
an entire EV brand, you can watch this video.

